    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-2439",
      "knowledge_point_id": "dff5ec0b-1456-5231-94e1-0fc5923d6672",
      "knowledge_point": "Determining a Rate of Change for a Given Function at a Point",
      "problem": "The weight $w,$ in pounds, of a baby gorilla $t$ years after birth can be modeled by the function $w(t)=4{t}^{3}-{t}^{2}+15.$ What is the instantaneous rate of change of his weight when $t=1$ year?",
      "worked_solution": "The instantaneous rate of change of weight when $t=1$ year is equal to ${w}^{′}(1).$\n\nFirst, we take the derivative and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{w}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4{t}^{3}-{t}^{2}+15) \\\\  & =12{t}^{2}-2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $t=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{w}^{′}(1) & =12(1)^{2}-2(1) \\\\  & =12-2 \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate change of the weight when $t=1$ year is $10$ pounds per year.",
