    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-48742",
      "knowledge_point_id": "dff5ec0b-1456-5231-94e1-0fc5923d6672",
      "knowledge_point": "Determining a Rate of Change for a Given Function at a Point",
      "problem": "The function $P(v)=\\frac{2v+50}{v+1}$ models the price at which gas is sold, in dollars, depending on the volume $v$ sold the previous day, in gallons. What is the instantaneous rate of change of the price of gas when $v=5$ gallons?",
      "worked_solution": "The instantaneous rate of change of the price when $v=5$ gallons is equal to ${P}^{′}(5).$\n\nNow, we take the derivative and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(v) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}v}(\\frac{2v+50}{v+1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}v}(2v+50)\\cdot (v+1)-(2v+50)\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}v}(v+1)}{(v+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(2)\\cdot (v+1)-(2v+50)\\cdot (1)}{(v+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2v+2-2v-50}{(v+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{48}{(v+1)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $v=5$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(5) & =-\\frac{48}{((5)+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{48}{36} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate change of the price when $v=5$ gallons is $-\\frac{4}{3}$ dollars per gallon.",
      "answer_fields": [
